In the first quadrant the arm is already acute, so $A=\theta$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (X) at (1.1,0);
\coordinate (Xn) at (-1.1,0);
\coordinate (P) at (50:2.4);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.1,0)--(3.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3.1)--(0,3.1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (O) circle (2.4);
\draw[thick, blue] (O)--(P);
\fill (P) circle (1.6pt);
\pic[draw, blue, "$\theta$", angle radius=1.35cm, angle eccentricity=1.55] {angle=X--O--P};
\pic[draw, red, "$A$", angle radius=0.65cm, angle eccentricity=2.3] {angle=X--O--P};
\node[anchor=west] at (-3.1,3.5) {Quadrant 1};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In the second quadrant the arm is measured back to the negative $x$-axis, so $A=180^{\circ}-\theta$.

